; the horizontal surface above — opposite of floor.
; an object can only reach the ceiling if it has enough upward velocity to
; overcome gravity: v² ≥ 2gh where h is the ceiling height.
; collision with ceiling: if object reaches it, normal force is downward.
; rarely collided with in everyday life (except thrown objects, bouncing balls).
; upper boundary of a room — defines maximum height for trajectory problems.
; 
; the ceiling IS the mathematical ceil function made spatial.
; math ceil(x) = smallest integer ≥ x — the upper bound that cannot be exceeded.
; physical ceiling = the spatial upper bound that cannot be exceeded.
; the abheda: both truncate upward. the ball's height cannot exceed the ceiling.
; x cannot exceed ceil(x) in integer space.
; floor and ceiling together define the interval — the bounded kshetra.
; math abheda: the ceil operation is this structure in number space

(kosha ceiling
  (vishesa structures-varga common-sense-varga)
  (swarupa horizontal overhead upper-boundary)
  (yukta normal-force collision-varga domain-3d)
  (sthita gravity)
  (abheda ceil)
  (pratipaksha floor))
